Fade the output in and out at the edges of a stream, and let a stop play out.

Stopping Spotify Connect through Music Assistant clicked: recorded at the
PipeWire sink, the music went from full level to exact zero in one sample.
Two things caused it. The queue running dry padded with zeros straight away,
a step from the last sample to 0; and clear() stopped the stream at once,
which discards whatever is still buffered between here and PipeWire, so even
a fade written into the padding was never heard.

Now padding continues from the last frame output and decays to silence
(-60 dB in 5 ms), the drop path takes the next step of that decay instead of
a zero, and clear() mid-music waits for the callback to write the fade and
for the output latency to play it before stopping (at most ~0.8 s). The
other way round, output going from silence to sound - a track start, a
resume - is ramped up over 10 ms. Silence for that purpose is 32 zero
frames, so music crossing zero on both channels does not dip.

The last frame survives clear() for that fade, so a format change drops
it when its size no longer fits: a sync insert would otherwise write an
old-size frame into the new stream's buffer.

